# .clinerules — Trading Bot Projects
# Applies to: backtest_framework.py, Apex_oracle_bot, Apex_Committee_Bot

## Hard rules — never do these without explicit, in-the-moment approval

- Never commit or push to any of these repos on your own. Stage changes and
  show me the diff — I approve the commit and push myself.
- Never modify `.env`, `.env.example` values, API keys, or anything in
  `docker-compose.yml` related to credentials (POSTGRES_PASSWORD,
  APCA_API_KEY_ID, APCA_API_SECRET_KEY, GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN).
  If a credential needs to change, tell me — don't change it yourself.
- Never run a command that places a live trade, cancels a live order, or
  hits a real Alpaca endpoint outside of paper trading / backtesting mode.
  If you're unsure whether an endpoint is live or paper, stop and ask.
- Never run `docker compose down -v` or any command that deletes a volume.
- Never delete files outside the specific repo you're working in.
- Never claim a file was created, modified, or verified without actually
  showing the command output that proves it (e.g. dir, Test-Path, cat).
  A description of what you did is not proof — the tool output is.

## Before touching these files, explain your plan first and wait for a go-ahead

- `src/exchange.py` / `exchange.py` — this is the live connection to the
  real exchange. Treat every change here as high-stakes by default.
- `src/risk.py` / `risk.py` — the safety layer between the bot and real
  money.
- `strategies.py` / `committee.py` / `brains/` — the actual trading logic.
- `database.py` / `db.py` — schema changes should be additive, not
  destructive.

## Definition of "done"

A task isn't finished just because the code runs once. Before saying a task
is complete:

1. Run the relevant test suite (`pytest tests/`) and confirm it passes, and
   show me the actual output.
2. Run Ruff and confirm no new lint errors were introduced.
3. Summarize what changed and why, in plain terms, before I review the diff.

## When using MCP tools

- Use the postgres/sqlite MCP servers to verify claims about real data —
  don't guess or extrapolate from code alone when the data is one query
  away.
- Use sequential-thinking for anything touching risk logic, position
  sizing, or multi-file refactors.
- If something surprising turns up, tell me — don't quietly work around it.

## General tone

- If you're not sure whether something is safe to do autonomously vs. needs
  my sign-off, default to asking.
